<a name="savepoints"/><h1>savepoints</h1>

<p>The savepoints module wraps each query in a database savepoint, and rolls back to it if the query fails, so the failure doesn't abort the surrounding transaction.  This is most useful with databases (eg. PostgreSQL) where a failed query otherwise causes every later query in the transaction to fail until the transaction is rolled back.</p>

<p>The trigger only takes savepoints inside a transaction.  Queries run in autocommit mode are passed through untouched, since a savepoint outside a transaction has no effect.  begin, commit, rollback, and autocommit-toggle queries are also passed through untouched.</p>

<p>An example configuration:</p>

<blockquote>
<!-- Generator: GNU source-highlight 3.1.9
by Lorenzo Bettini
http://www.lorenzobettini.it
http://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite -->
<pre><tt><b><font color="#000080">&lt;?xml</font></b> <font color="#009900">version</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1.0"</font><b><font color="#000080">?&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instances&gt;</font></b>
	...
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instance</font></b> <font color="#009900">id</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"example"</font> ... <b><font color="#0000FF">&gt;</font></b>
		...
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;triggers&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;trigger</font></b> <font color="#009900">module</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"savepoints"</font>
				<font color="#009900">when</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"all"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/triggers&gt;</font></b>
		...
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instance&gt;</font></b>
	...
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instances&gt;</font></b>
</tt></pre>

</blockquote>
<p>In this example, before every non-transaction-control query that runs inside a transaction, the trigger runs <i>savepoint sqlrspN</i> (where <i>N</i> is an auto-incrementing counter).  If the user's query succeeds, the trigger runs <i>release savepoint sqlrspN</i> to keep the savepoint stack bounded.  If the user's query fails, the trigger runs <i>rollback to savepoint sqlrspN</i> to undo the failed query.  Either way, the user's error (if any) is preserved and reported back to the client - the trigger contains the failure but doesn't hide it.</p>

<p>The trigger should be configured with <i>when="all"</i>, so it wraps both the prepare and execute phases.  Some databases (eg. PostgreSQL) report errors like a missing table when the query is prepared, not when it runs.</p>

<p>The default savepoint, rollback, and release syntax is standard SQL and works for PostgreSQL, MySQL/MariaDB, Oracle, DB2, Firebird, and other databases that follow the SQL standard.  For databases with non-standard savepoint syntax (e.g. Microsoft SQL Server, SAP/Sybase ASE), the syntax templates are configurable:</p>

<blockquote>
<!-- Generator: GNU source-highlight 3.1.9
by Lorenzo Bettini
http://www.lorenzobettini.it
http://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite -->
<pre><tt><b><font color="#000080">&lt;?xml</font></b> <font color="#009900">version</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1.0"</font><b><font color="#000080">?&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instances&gt;</font></b>
	...
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instance</font></b> <font color="#009900">id</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"example"</font> ... <b><font color="#0000FF">&gt;</font></b>
		...
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;triggers&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;trigger</font></b> <font color="#009900">module</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"savepoints"</font>
				<font color="#009900">when</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"all"</font>
				<font color="#009900">savepointquery</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"save tran %s"</font>
				<font color="#009900">rollbackquery</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"rollback tran %s"</font>
				<font color="#009900">release</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"no"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/triggers&gt;</font></b>
		...
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instance&gt;</font></b>
	...
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instances&gt;</font></b>
</tt></pre>

</blockquote>
<p>The <b>trigger</b> tag supports the following attributes in addition to the standard <b>module</b>, <b>when</b>, <b>enabled</b>, and <b>debug</b> attributes:</p>

<ul>
  <li><b>savepointquery</b></li>
  <ul>
    <li>SQL template for creating a savepoint.  <i>%s</i> is replaced with the generated savepoint name.  Defaults to <i>savepoint %s</i>.</li>
  </ul>

  <li><b>rollbackquery</b></li>
  <ul>
    <li>SQL template for rolling back to a savepoint.  <i>%s</i> is replaced with the generated savepoint name.  Defaults to <i>rollback to savepoint %s</i>.</li>
  </ul>

  <li><b>releasequery</b></li>
  <ul>
    <li>SQL template for releasing a savepoint.  <i>%s</i> is replaced with the generated savepoint name.  Defaults to <i>release savepoint %s</i>.</li>
  </ul>

  <li><b>release</b></li>
  <ul>
    <li>If set to "yes" (the default), the trigger runs <i>releasequery</i> after each successful query, so the savepoint stack doesn't grow without bound for long-running transactions.  Set to "no" for databases that don't support releasing savepoints (e.g. MS SQL Server, SAP/Sybase).</li>
  </ul>

  <li><b>prefix</b></li>
  <ul>
    <li>Prefix used for the auto-generated savepoint names.  Defaults to <i>sqlrsp</i>.  Combined with a per-session counter to form names like <i>sqlrsp0</i>, <i>sqlrsp1</i>, and so on.</li>
  </ul>

</ul>

<br/><h2>Considerations</h2>

<p>There is a performance cost.  Every query inside a transaction generates two extra round-trips to the database (savepoint before, release or rollback after).  For workloads with short transactions or high query rates, this overhead can be significant.</p>

<p>The trigger does not retry the failed query.  After rolling back to the savepoint, the original error is still reported to the client; it's up to the application (or another trigger like <i>replay</i>) to decide whether to retry.</p>

